  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 955 PM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nAs the upper level ridge remains the main influence over the area\nthrough the long term, there will also be another feature to\nwatch. As an upper level shortwave and subsequent low pressure\nsystem shift eastward north of the Great Lakes, a weak ridge will\ndevelop ahead of this in the northern Appalachian region and bring\nsubsequent sfc high pressure. In turn, weak CAD will advect into\nthe eastern portions of the area, which will likely set up a\ndifferential heating boundary by early to mid week and allow low\nchances of thunderstorms (30% or less) each afternoon. This will\nbe dependent on where the sfc high officially sets up. Otherwise,\nmostly dry conditions are forecast with continued high\ntemperatures in the low to mid 90s. Beyond the long term, the\nClimate Prediction Center keeps us in an area for likely above\nnormal temperatures for the 8-14 day period. Therefore, relief\nfrom this heat is not currently in sight.",
